#X text 501 154 Defining and sizing arrays for gesture vector creation. Note that Yaw is not used \, but is left in there incase of future redesign., f 66;
#X text 217 560 Statistical analysis on circular buffers is performed every 100ms. This calculates the average Pitch/Roll and difference in Pitch/Roll over 200 data points \, as well as the maximum linear acceleration magnitude., f 87;
#X text -164 175 This counter loop sends IMU data to a new array index every time data is received. Once index 200 has been reached \, the counter resets back to 0 and old data is overwritten. Essentially a circular buffer., f 19;
#X text 286 847 Note that the average linear acceleration magnitude is available but is not currently being used., f 29;
#X text -393 96 The input vector is a gesture that you record by moving the Bela controller. Subtle gestures should map to short paths through the corpus \, and more agressive gestures to long paths., f 28;
#X text -393 207 Furthermore \, the path should correspond to the gesture in an interpretable way. For example \, a gesture that moves from left to right should correspond to a output path that also moves from left to right., f 28;
#X text -393 334 To record the input vector \, load this patch into the Bela IDE and rename it to "_main.pd". Note that a timer will countdown from 3 before any data is recorded \, giving you time to pick up the controller after it has calibrated., f 28;
#X text -92 597 UPDATE FILENAME BEFORE NEW RECORDING, f 20;
#X text 291 1136 UPDATE FILENAME BEFORE NEW RECORDING, f 37;
#X text -393 474 IMPORTANT - Make sure before recording to set a new identifier. The identifier should match the corresponding output dataset for the current input. The objects to update are identified in the patch: "makefilename x-x-%04d" and "write x-x-gestures.json". Unfortunately \, this must currently be done offline in pd and then re-uploaded to the Bela IDE every time you record a new gesture., f 28;
#X text 423 346 This logic is to ensure that the metronome only starts when data is received. When data is received \, a 3 second countdown begins before the gesture is recorded. This countdown is printed in the Bela IDE terminal. The device should not be picked up until this countdown begins to ensure that it finishes calibrating., f 25;
#X text -393 4 This patch is for creating input gesture vector datasets. These are later used to train the MLP Regressor interactive machine learning algorithm., f 28;
